# Exercise: if/else/then
#
# The `else` clause provides an alternative when the condition is false.
#
# Syntax:
#   condition if
#       code-if-true
#   else
#       code-if-false
#   then
#
# Important: Both branches must have the SAME stack effect!
#
# Remember: Seq has no variables. The condition and branches
# work directly with values on the stack.
#
# Example - take the absolute value of whatever is on top:
#   dup 0 i.>= if
#       # already non-negative, leave it
#   else
#       -1 i.*   # negate it
#   then
#
# (We used `dup` because the comparison consumed the value,
#  but we still want to use it in the branches.)
#
# Your task: If 3 is greater than 7, push 1, otherwise push 2.
# (Hint: 3 is NOT greater than 7, so push 2)
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-if-else ( -- )
    # Your code here
    # Is 3 > 7? If yes, push 1. If no, push 2.
    0
    # Do not edit below this line
    2 test.assert-eq
;
